# Architecture Principles (Simple)

Keep the architecture straightforward and maintainable with clear boundaries and minimal layers.

All business logic lives in services.

## Layers and Responsibilities

- Controllers: HTTP concerns only (routing, validation via Form Requests, mapping to resources)
- Services: Business orchestration and transactions; compose repositories and providers
- Repositories (DAL): Persistence and query logic; no business rules

## Dependency Direction

- Controller ΓåÆ Service ΓåÆ Repository ΓåÆ Database
- Depend on interfaces for external providers; bind implementations in service providers
- Avoid circular dependencies; prefer constructor injection

## Simplicity & Refactoring

- Start simple; avoid premature abstraction
- Refactor when real needs emerge (reuse, complexity, testing)

## When to Create a New Layer or Extract Logic

- Extract to a service when controller logic grows or is reused
- Extract to a repository when queries grow complex or shared
- Extract to a composable/helper when frontend or service logic is reusable and pure

## Code Organization

- Group by domain/feature where practical (e.g., Orders, Billing)
- Co-locate DTOs, services, repositories within their domain
- Keep files small and focused; prefer many small units over large god-classes

## Testing

- Unit test services with mocked repositories/providers
- Integration test DAL with database
- Contract test public interfaces (APIs) against schemas

Keep it simple: thin controllers, cohesive services, focused repositories.

